    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p10-p1-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Let $t=\\tan15^\\circ$. Show that $\\dfrac{2t}{1-t^2}=\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{2t}{1-t^2}=\\tan30^\\circ=\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$.",
          "worked": "Substitute $\\theta=15^\\circ$ and $t=\\tan15^\\circ$ into the supplied identity:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{2t}{1-t^2}&=\\tan(2\\times15^\\circ)\\\\\\frac{2t}{1-t^2}&=\\tan30^\\circ\\\\\\frac{2t}{1-t^2}&=\\frac1{\\sqrt3}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-p1-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that $t^2+2\\sqrt3t-1=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2\\sqrt3t=1-t^2$, so $t^2+2\\sqrt3t-1=0$.",
          "worked": "Multiply the equation in part (a) by $\\sqrt3(1-t^2)$:\n$$\\begin{align*}2\\sqrt3t&=1-t^2\\\\t^2+2\\sqrt3t-1&=0.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-p1-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence, show that $\\tan15^\\circ=2-\\sqrt3$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\tan15^\\circ=2-\\sqrt3$.\n\nSolve the quadratic and choose the positive root.",
          "worked": "Using the quadratic formula,\n$$\\begin{align*}t&=\\frac{-2\\sqrt3\\pm\\sqrt{(2\\sqrt3)^2-4(1)(-1)}}2\\\\t&=\\frac{-2\\sqrt3\\pm4}2\\\\t&=-\\sqrt3\\pm2.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $15^\\circ$ is acute, $t=\\tan15^\\circ>0$. Therefore $\\tan15^\\circ=2-\\sqrt3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-p1-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Use a similar technique to show that $\\tan22.5^\\circ=\\sqrt2-1$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\tan22.5^\\circ=\\sqrt2-1$.\n\nSubstitute $22.5^\\circ$ into the supplied identity and choose the positive quadratic root.",
          "worked": "Let $u=\\tan22.5^\\circ$. The supplied identity gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{2u}{1-u^2}&=\\tan45^\\circ\\\\\\frac{2u}{1-u^2}&=1\\\\2u&=1-u^2\\\\u^2+2u-1&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nUsing the quadratic formula,\n$$\\begin{align*}u&=\\frac{-2\\pm\\sqrt{2^2-4(1)(-1)}}2\\\\u&=\\frac{-2\\pm2\\sqrt2}2\\\\u&=-1\\pm\\sqrt2.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $22.5^\\circ$ is acute, $u>0$. Therefore $\\tan22.5^\\circ=\\sqrt2-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
  },
